Yes. The joke is that “literal” itself has layers, and you had been treating one of them metaphorically.

What you’re describing is closer to frame-relative literalism: a person is not necessarily failing to understand reality; they may be interpreting observations through a different ontology, vocabulary, causal model, or cultural frame. Inside that frame, their conclusions can feel completely literal and self-evident.

So instead of:

\text{same world} \rightarrow \text{same representation}

you really have:

\text{shared reality}
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
F_A(\text{reality})\\
F_B(\text{reality})\\
F_C(\text{reality})
\end{cases}

Each F is a different perceptual/conceptual transformation.

And crucially, you have one too. Your frame feels less like a frame precisely because you are standing inside it.

That gives you a better way to “map” another perspective. Not mind-reading, but reconstruction:

\text{observations}
+
\text{language}
+
\text{history}
+
\text{values}
+
\text{environment}
+
\text{causal assumptions}
\rightarrow
\text{candidate world-model}

Then you test whether that model predicts how they interpret new things.

If it does, you have started reconstructing the geometry of their frame.

There are several useful dimensions to map:

* What counts as an object?
* What counts as a cause?
* Where are the boundaries between self, family, nature, spirit, institution, etc.?
* Which things are taken literally, symbolically, relationally, or simultaneously?
* What evidence changes belief?
* Which categories do they have that you lack?
* Which categories do you have that they do not?

That last pair matters a lot. Sometimes translation fails not because either side is stupid, but because one ontology contains a distinction the other has never needed to make.

You can then do the really interesting maneuver:

F_A \rightarrow \text{abstract relation} \rightarrow F_B

Instead of translating words, translate the underlying relation.

For example, a culture may not package “ecosystem feedback” as a scientific term, yet have stories about violating a seasonal taboo and the land ceasing to provide. Those are not automatically scientifically equivalent explanations—but they may encode overlapping relationships between behavior, environment, and consequence.

That is where your earlier operator-intersection rule protects us: find the overlap, then perturb around it to determine how deep the equivalence really goes.

And “see and feel them” can become more precise too:

see = reconstruct their representational frame;
feel = model the emotional consequences of events within that frame;
test = compare the model with what people actually report and do.

You never fully become the other perspective, but you can move much farther than merely translating their sentences into your own categories.

Which leads to another good Pantheon operator:

\boxed{\text{Frame Rotation}}

Hold reality approximately fixed, rotate the interpretive coordinate system, and observe which relationships remain invariant and which ones appear, disappear, reverse, or become salient.

Those invariants are especially interesting.

Because that is where you may have found something beneath either worldview rather than merely inside one of them.